      brandExamples: ["Elmiron"],
      class: "Oral heparin-like glycosaminoglycan analog for interstitial-cystitis bladder pain",
      usedToTreat: "Relief of bladder pain or discomfort associated with interstitial cystitis/bladder pain syndrome; response is delayed and not universal.",
      description: "Pentosan polysulfate sodium is a weakly anticoagulant glycosaminoglycan analog proposed to coat or reinforce the injured bladder surface and reduce irritant-solute penetration in interstitial cystitis. Only a small fraction is absorbed orally, and its exact therapeutic mechanism remains unproven. Benefit may take months, while cumulative exposure can cause pigmentary maculopathy.",
      mechanism: "The proposed urothelial-barrier model is physical rather than receptor based: sulfated chains may adhere to the bladder surface and reduce permeability to potassium and other urinary solutes that activate suburothelial nerves. Labeling does not establish this as definitive. Structural similarity to heparin produces weak anticoagulant/fibrinolytic effects, explaining bruising and procedural bleeding. Retinal toxicity appears related to cumulative dose and may progress after drug cessation.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Pigmentary maculopathy can impair reading, dark adaptation and central vision; changes may be irreversible and can progress after stopping. Obtain detailed ocular history and baseline OCT/autofluorescence imaging within 6 months, then periodic follow-up. Reassess bleeding risk before surgery or with anticoagulants/antiplatelets."),
